          "tool_name": "write_file",
          "input_json": "{\u0022path\u0022:\u0022pages/concepts/retrieval-patterns.md\u0022,\u0022content\u0022:\u0022---\\ntype: Concept\\ntitle: Retrieval Patterns\\ndescription: Techniques for combining sparse lexical and dense vector search to improve information retrieval, especially in RAG systems.\\ntimestamp: 2026-07-14T00:00:00Z\\ntags:\\n  - concept/Retrieval\\n  - concept/Ranking\\n  - pattern/RAG\\nconfidence: medium\\nconfidence_reason: \\u0022One authoritative source describing hybrid retrieval; emerging standard in RAG systems but not yet corroborated by independent sources.\\u0022\\n---\\n\\n## Hybrid Retrieval\\n\\nHybrid retrieval combines sparse lexical search (such as BM25) with dense vector search, then merges the two ranked result sets to capture both exact term matches and semantic similarity. The merging step typically uses reciprocal rank fusion, which balances contributions from both methods.\\n\\n### Performance Characteristics\\n\\nHybrid retrieval consistently outperforms either method alone on queries that mix rare identifiers (error codes, product SKUs) with natural language intent. This makes it particularly effective for real-world RAG applications where users may search for both structured data and conceptual information in the same query.\\n\\n### Adoption\\n\\nHybrid retrieval has become the default retrieval pattern in production RAG systems, reflecting its practical effectiveness across diverse query types.\\n\\n## Citations\\n\\n[^1]: Hybrid retrieval source (eval://update-over-duplicate/sample-07) \\u2014 combining sparse and dense search methods in production RAG.\\n\u0022}"
